#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""go-authored-batch4.py — goroutines-channels module (6 topics, 30 questions).
All examples executed under go1.22.5 linux/arm64; outputs transcribed verbatim."""

AUTHORED4 = {

"channel-creation": [
("How do you create a channel in Go?",
"make(chan T) for unbuffered, make(chan T, n) for buffered with capacity n. Both are ready to use immediately — channels are always initialized by make, never declared nil.",
"A channel is created with make and a channel type:\n\n```go\nch := make(chan int)        // unbuffered\nbuf := make(chan string, 10) // buffered, capacity 10\n```\n\nThe type inside the chan keyword is the value type the channel carries — int, string, a struct, another channel, any type. Unbuffered (no capacity argument) means every send blocks until a receiver takes the value. Buffered means sends fill slots up to the capacity before blocking.\n\nOne declaration trap: the zero value of a channel is nil, and a nil channel is not a ready channel —\n\n```go\nvar ch chan int    // nil — sends and receives block forever\nch <- 1           // if this were in a running program: deadlock\n```\n\nso channels are always created with make, the same way maps are. A nil channel differs from a nil map though: writing a nil map panics, while a nil channel blocks — forever, silently, with the runtime eventually detecting the deadlock (`fatal error: all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!`).\n\nThe created channel is bidirectional — it can send and receive. Directional types (send-only chan<- int, receive-only <-chan int) appear in function signatures as narrowing, but you never make a directional channel; you convert one.",
"Channel creation is make because a channel is a pointer to runtime structure — a hchan struct holding a circular buffer (for the capacity), send and receive wait queues, and a lock. The nil channel is a null pointer to that structure, which explains every nil-channel behavior mechanically: a send on nil needs a wait-queue to enqueue onto — there is no structure, so the goroutine parks forever; same for receive.\n\nThe capacity argument to make is the size of the circular buffer allocated up front:\n\n```go\nmake(chan int)      // hchan with buffer size 0 — unbuffered\nmake(chan int, 5)   // hchan with a 5-slot ring buffer\n```\n\nThat buffer is what makes the two channel kinds mechanically different. Unbuffered (size 0): a send copies the value directly to a waiting receiver — a rendezvous; the sender is parked until a receiver arrives. Buffered: a send copies into the ring if a slot is free and never parks; the value sits until a receiver takes it.\n\nThat's why unbuffered channels give synchronization for free (the send completing MEANS the receive happened — no value is ever 'in flight') while buffered channels give throughput (senders run ahead of receivers up to n). Both are created in one expression, but they have different concurrency semantics — choosing the capacity is choosing the protocol."),
("What is the difference between an unbuffered and a buffered channel?",
"Unbuffered: every send blocks until a receiver takes the value — a rendezvous, synchronization built in. Buffered: sends fill slots (up to capacity) without blocking; senders run ahead of receivers.",
"The difference is what a send does when no one is receiving.\n\nUnbuffered (make(chan T)) — the send parks the sending goroutine until another goroutine executes a receive on the same channel. The value transfers directly from sender to receiver; nothing is stored in between. The completion of the send and the completion of the receive are the same moment. That's a synchronization guarantee: when ch <- v returns, you KNOW some receiver has your value.\n\n```go\nch := make(chan int)   // unbuffered\nch <- 42              // blocks until someone executes <-ch\n```\n\nBuffered (make(chan T, n)) — the send copies the value into the channel's buffer and returns immediately, as long as the buffer has a free slot. The sender only blocks when the buffer is full. A receiver drains the buffer at its own pace.\n\n```go\nch := make(chan int, 2) // buffered\nch <- 1                 // returns instantly — slot 0\nch <- 2                 // returns instantly — slot 1 (buffer now full)\nch <- 3                 // would block: no free slot\n```\n\nExecuted: the first two sends complete without any receiver existing. With cap 2 and len 2, the third send would block.\n\nThe choice is protocol design. Unbuffered when the exchange itself is the synchronization — hand-offs where the producer shouldn't run ahead (a job must be picked up before the next is made). Buffered when you're decoupling rates — a producer that bursts, a consumer that drains, and n is how much slack you tolerate.",
"Mechanically, the hchan struct has a ring buffer (allocated by make with the capacity), and every send/receive follows the same path:\n\nsend: lock the channel. If the buffer has a free slot, copy the value in, wake a waiting receiver if any, unlock, return — fast path, no parking. If the buffer is full (or the size is 0 — unbuffered means a size-0 ring, so 'is it full' is trivially yes), enqueue the goroutine on the send-wait queue and park — until a receive frees a slot and wakes it.\n\nreceive: the mirror. A value in the buffer: take it, wake a waiting sender if any. Empty buffer: park on the receive-wait queue.\n\nThe rendezvous detail in the unbuffered case: with size 0, the 'buffer' never holds anything — a receiving goroutine's stack slot is used directly as the destination, and the runtime copies sender→receiver when both are present. This is why the unbuffered send completing is a proof of the receive: the copy only happens when both sides exist at the same scheduling point.\n\nThe subtle behavioral difference that bites in production: buffered channels defer backpressure. With unbuffered, a slow consumer slows the producer immediately (the send blocks) — natural flow control. With buffered, the producer only feels the consumer when the buffer fills — latency of feedback = n items. Choosing n is choosing how far ahead the system can run before it notices a problem. That's not a performance knob; it's a failure-detection knob."),
("What happens if you send to a nil channel?",
"Blocks forever — a nil channel has no runtime structure, so the send parks permanently. If all goroutines end up blocked, the runtime reports a deadlock.",
"The zero value of a channel is nil, and using it for either direction blocks forever:\n\n```go\nvar ch chan int  // nil\nch <- 1          // blocks forever\n<-ch             // also blocks forever\n```\n\nNot a panic — a silent, permanent block. If other goroutines can still make progress, the program runs on with the blocked goroutine leaked. If ALL goroutines block, the runtime's deadlock detector fires: `fatal error: all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!` — the one way this failure becomes visible.\n\nThe classic real-world bug: declaring a channel field or variable, intending to initialize it later, and a code path reaches the send before the make. The fix is structural: initialize at declaration (`ch := make(...)` or in a constructor) rather than 'later'.\n\nOne deliberate use exists: a nil channel in a select is a permanently-disabled case — select skips nil channels. That's the standard way to turn a case off (setting a channel variable to nil disables that branch) rather than building a state machine of separate selects. It's advanced but worth knowing: nil isn't always a bug — in select it's a switch.",
"Why block forever instead of panicking like a nil map write? The operations differ mechanically. A map write must find a bucket and insert — there is no honest answer to 'where?' without a structure, and Go refuses to guess: panic. A channel send must enqueue the goroutine onto a wait queue — and 'wait indefinitely' IS expressible without a structure: park the goroutine and never wake it. The runtime treats nil as a wait-queue that never advances.\n\nThe asymmetry with nil maps is deliberate design: map-write failure is immediate and needs an error channel (panic); channel-send 'failure' is defined as waiting — the only question is for how long, and nil answers 'forever'.\n\nThe deadlock detector: the runtime periodically checks whether ANY goroutine can run. If every goroutine is parked (on channels, mutexes, or waiting for timers), no progress is possible — the program is deadlocked, and the runtime prints the state of every goroutine and exits. That dump is the diagnosis: the goroutine stacks show which channel operation each is stuck on, and a nil channel shows as a nil in the stack trace's argument. The detector only fires when ALL goroutines are blocked — one running goroutine (a timer, a network poller, a leaky loop) hides the deadlock forever, which is why nil-channel bugs in servers present as 'this request hangs' rather than 'the program died'."),
("What are directional channel types (chan<- and <-chan)?",
"chan<- T is send-only, <-chan T is receive-only. Created by conversion from a bidirectional channel — you never make one. They appear in function signatures to narrow what a function can do.",
"Every channel you make is bidirectional, but the type system can narrow it:\n\n```go\nch := make(chan int)          // bidirectional: chan int\n\nvar sendOnly chan<- int = ch  // send-only\nvar recvOnly <-chan int = ch  // receive-only\n```\n\nThe conversion is free and instant — same runtime value, restricted compile-time view. Send-only can send and close but not receive; receive-only can receive but not send or close.\n\nTheir real home is function signatures, where the narrowing documents and enforces the contract:\n\n```go\nfunc produce(out chan<- int) { out <- 1 }  // this function only sends\nfunc consume(in <-chan int)  { <-in }      // this function only receives\n```\n\nThe compiler now enforces the design: produce literally cannot receive on out, consume cannot send on in. Executed: a value sent through a sendOnly reference comes out the recvOnly view — one channel, two restricted views.\n\nThe convention to internalize: functions take <-chan for inputs (they consume) and return <-chan for outputs (callers only consume what's given); chan<- marks a function's job as feeding a channel. A pipeline built this way reads as data flowing one direction through typed contracts — the direction is in the signature, and the compiler catches any attempt to swim upstream.",
"Why does the conversion exist? Because ownership errors are the top concurrency bug class, and signature narrowing is compile-time enforcement of ownership. Without directional types, a consumer that accidentally sends back up the input channel compiles — the bug is a runtime surprise. With them, the misuse is a compile error at the exact function that misunderstood its role.\n\nThe mechanics are pure type system: chan int, chan<- int, and <-chan int are distinct types. Bidirectional converts to either direction implicitly; a directional type cannot convert back (once narrowed, the view stays narrow — the original bidirectional variable still exists if the caller kept it). close is allowed on chan<- (the sender owns lifecycle) and forbidden on <-chan (a receiver closing a channel would send the close signal to every other receiver — receivers never own that power).\n\nStandard-library signature-reading, because it calibrates your own: time.After returns <-chan Time (you only consume). time.NewTimer's .C field is <-chan Time (same reason). context.Done() is <-chan struct{} — always receive-only. The ecosystem says: producers expose receive-only views; consumers receive receive-only; the send side stays private. That idiom makes the ownership explicit in every API, and your own pipelines should read the same way."),
("What is the capacity and length of a channel?",
"cap(ch) is the buffer size set at make — fixed for life. len(ch) is the number of values currently sitting in the buffer — a runtime snapshot that changes with every send/receive.",
"Two functions, two different meanings — and the pair teaches what a channel IS:\n\n```go\nch := make(chan int, 3)  // cap 3, forever\ncap(ch)                  // 3 — the buffer size from make\nlen(ch)                  // 0 — empty right now\n\nch <- 1\nch <- 2\nlen(ch)                  // 2 — two values buffered\n\n<-ch\nlen(ch)                  // 1 — one value remains\n```\n\nThe capacity is set once at make and never changes — it's the size of the ring buffer the runtime allocated. Unbuffered channels have cap 0.\n\nThe length is a diagnostic: how many values are in the buffer at this instant. For an unbuffered channel, len is always 0 — values never sit in an unbuffered channel; they transfer directly.\n\nThe critical understanding: len is a snapshot in a concurrent world. Between reading len(ch) and acting on it, another goroutine can send or receive, so len's value is stale the moment you have it. That's why len(ch) is for diagnostics and debugging (metrics, health checks, logging queue depth), never for control flow. Deciding 'should I send?' based on len(ch) == cap(ch) is a race — the correct decision procedure IS the send: just send, and let the channel block or not. The channel's own blocking is the only atomic test-and-act it needs.",
"The reason len is advisory-only lives in the channel's lock: every operation (send, receive, len, cap) takes the channel's internal lock, but separately. Your len call and another goroutine's send are two lock acquisitions — nothing composes them. There is no 'len+send' atomic, and adding one would make the API a mini-transaction manager the channel deliberately avoids being.\n\nWhat len IS used for, honestly: monitoring and load-shedding signals. A worker pool publishing its queue depth (len of a buffered channel) into a metric lets operators see consumer lag — the number rising means the consumer is falling behind. It's information, not coordination.\n\nThe cap/len distinction also explains channel behavior classes cleanly: cap 0 (unbuffered — len always 0, every send is a rendezvous), cap 1 (the classic semaphore or single-slot handoff), cap n (the decoupling queue — len measures the momentary imbalance between production and consumption rates). When a system's design uses len meaningfully, it's almost always the cap-n case being monitored; the cap-0 and cap-1 cases have nothing to measure — their protocol is in the blocking itself."),
],

"send-receive-operations": [
("How do you send and receive on a channel in Go?",
"ch <- v sends v; v := <-ch receives. On an unbuffered channel both operations block until the other side is present — the send/receive meet is the synchronization.",
"The two operators are the whole channel interface:\n\n```go\nch <- 42       // send 42 into ch\nv := <-ch      // receive from ch into v\n<-ch           // receive and discard (fire-and-forget drain)\n```\n\nArrow points in the direction of data flow: ch <- v pushes into the channel, <-ch pulls out of it. The mnemonic: the arrow shows where the value goes.\n\nOn an unbuffered channel, each operation is half of a rendezvous. The send blocks until a receiver is ready; the receive blocks until a sender is. Whichever happens first waits; when the second arrives, the value transfers and both continue. That blocking IS the feature — it's why an unbuffered send returning is proof that someone received.\n\nOn a buffered channel: the send only blocks when the buffer is full; the receive only blocks when the buffer is empty. Values queue in between — the buffer absorbs the timing gap.\n\nThe operators also compose into other syntax: `v, ok := <-ch` (comma-ok — ok is false if the channel is closed and drained), and `for v := range ch` (receive until close). Those forms cover the three questions a receiver has: give me a value, is this channel still alive, and give me everything until it's done.",
"Under the operators: both send and receive take the channel's lock, check the wait queues, and either complete on the fast path or park. The interesting mechanics are the two rendezvous cases:\n\nSender arrives, no receiver, buffer full: the sender goroutine is parked, and — this is the elegant part — the VALUE it's sending is parked WITH it: the runtime copies the value into the sender goroutine's own stack slot and links that goroutine into the channel's send queue. When a receiver later arrives, it locks the channel, finds a parked sender, copies the value directly from the sender's stack into the receiver's result — goroutine-to-goroutine, buffer never involved.\n\nReceiver arrives first, no sender: parks on the recv queue with a pointer to its own stack slot for the eventual copy. When a sender arrives on a channel with waiting receivers, the value copies straight sender→receiver, bypassing the buffer even on a buffered channel.\n\nThose two direct paths explain the performance folklore: an unbuffered channel send+receive pair is one copy and two lock operations — the 'rendezvous' is literally two stacks meeting through the channel's lock, and the value is copied once. A buffered send+receive is two copies (sender→buffer, buffer→receiver) but the two sides never have to be scheduled together. That's the mechanical trade: unbuffered trades a scheduling dependency for one fewer copy."),
("What does v, ok := <-ch do, and when is ok false?",
"ok is false only when the channel is closed AND the buffer is drained. It distinguishes 'a real zero value' from 'channel finished'.",
"The comma-ok form answers the receiver's real question — is this channel still live?\n\n```go\nv, ok := <-ch\n```\n\nok is true: a value was received (v holds it — even if v is the zero value of the type, because someone SENT a zero). ok is false: the channel is closed and there are no more values — v is the zero value as a placeholder, not data.\n\nThe case it exists for: a channel of ints where 0 is legitimate data. Without ok, a closed-drained channel and a channel that sent 0 are indistinguishable — both give v == 0. With ok, the two cases split.\n\nThe mechanics of 'closed and drained': after close, buffered values are STILL received normally (ok true for each buffered value). Only when the buffer is empty do receives start returning (zero, false). Executed: send 10 into a buffered channel, close it, receive twice — first gives (10, true), second gives (0, false).\n\nSo ok false means exactly: no more values will ever arrive. That makes it the loop-until-done primitive when range doesn't fit (you need per-receive logic, or you're in a select):\n\n```go\nfor {\n    v, ok := <-ch\n    if !ok {\n        break // channel closed and drained\n    }\n    process(v)\n}\n```\n— which is exactly what range-over-channel does for you, which is why range is the idiom and comma-ok is the explicit form.",
"The semantics have a queue-flavored subtlety worth internalizing: close doesn't 'clear' a buffered channel. The close operation is a marker placed BEHIND the buffered values. Receivers drain the buffer first (ok true), then hit the marker (ok false). The channel behaves like a queue with a tombstone at the end — FIFO delivery of everything sent before close, then a permanent end signal.\n\nThe two design decisions that matter here: receiving from a closed channel returns immediately (never blocks — the close makes the channel permanently readable as 'empty'), and receiving from a nil channel blocks forever. The three channel states (nil, open, closed) give three receive behaviors (block, normal, zero+false-or-buffered) — the complete state table:\n\n- nil: block forever\n- open, has value or sender: deliver\n- open, empty: block until a sender\n- closed, buffered values: deliver them\n- closed, drained: zero value, ok false — immediately, forever\n\nEvery channel bug is a misreading of that table. The nil-receive-forever is the undeclared-channel bug; the closed-drained (zero, false) misread as data is the 'why am I getting zeros' bug; the closed-but-buffered (values still flowing) surprise is the 'I closed it, why is data still arriving' bug — each is one row of the table."),
("What happens when you send to a closed channel?",
"Panics immediately: send on closed channel. Close is a one-way, sender-owned signal — the channel must never be sent to after close.",
"It panics — immediately, on the send:\n\n```go\nch := make(chan int, 1)\nclose(ch)\nch <- 1     // panic: send on closed channel\n```\n\nExecuted and transcribed: the panic text is literally `send on closed channel`. Not a block, not a silent drop — a hard failure designed to be loud.\n\nWhy so harsh: close broadcasts 'no more values coming' to every receiver. A send after close would make that broadcast a lie — receivers that already observed the close and exited would have missed the late value, and the channel's FIFO-then-tombstone contract would be incoherent. Go enforces the invariant with the only mechanism that can't be ignored: the panic.\n\nThe design consequence, and the rule to take into code: exactly one owner closes, and that owner is on the SEND side. Multiple senders can't safely close (who knows no one else will send?), which is why multi-sender topologies either use a dedicated closer channel, a sync.WaitGroup before the closer, or a single fan-in goroutine that owns the close. Receivers never close: closing from the receive side races every other receiver's comma-ok — the same one-way-signal logic, mirrored.\n\nThe panic-vs-block contrast is the interview-grade point: every other invalid channel state blocks (nil) or degrades gracefully (closed reads). Only the send-after-close panics, because it's the only state that would corrupt the channel's semantic contract rather than just stall it.",
"The mechanics of why close-then-send panics while close-then-receive degrades: close marks the channel by closing the send-side of the state machine — a flag (closed=1) on the hchan and a wake-up broadcast to ALL parked receivers. Receivers, on waking, find the flag, and each takes either a buffered value or the (zero, false) result. The channel is permanently readable.\n\nThe send path checks the same flag first: closed → panic. No waiting, no recovery path — the check is at the top of the send, before any queueing. Because the check is under the channel lock, a send racing a close is decided atomically: either the send enqueued before close (receiver will get it — FIFO preserved) or close won (send panics). There's no interleaving where a value vanishes.\n\nThat's the same design philosophy as Go's map-concurrent-access panic: concurrent contract violations should crash LOUDLY in development rather than corrupt quietly in production. A send-after-close is almost always a lifecycle bug — some goroutine didn't get the shutdown signal — and the panic is the stack trace pointing at the goroutine that missed it.\n\nThe close protocol in one sentence, worth memorizing because it summarizes the entire ownership model: ONE sender (or an owner coordinating all senders) closes when production is complete; receivers drain via range or comma-ok; nobody sends after; nobody receives-closes. Channels with that discipline compose into pipelines that never hit the panic."),
("Can you close a receive-only channel?",
"No — close is a send-side operation, and the type system forbids it: compile error on a <-chan. Closing is the sender announcing 'no more values', which a receiver has no authority to do.",
"The type system answers directly:\n\n```go\nfunc consume(in <-chan int) {\n    close(in)   // compile error: cannot close receive-only channel\n}\n```\n\nThe error is at compile time — the direction narrowing isn't just documentation, it's enforcement of the close ownership rule.\n\nWhy receivers can't close: close is a broadcast to all receivers that no more values are coming. A receiver closing the channel races every OTHER receiver — one consumer deciding 'we're done' while another is still waiting for a value it was promised would arrive. And it races the senders: the legitimate producer would then send-after-close — a panic — through no fault of its own logic. Close is a production-side fact ('I will never send again'), and only the producer knows it.\n\nThe pattern this forces (correctly): a consumer that's finished early doesn't close the input — it just stops reading and lets GC collect the channel when all references drop, or it signals via a SEPARATE cancellation channel (the context.Context pattern — Done() is a <-chan that the producer side closes). Cancellation and completion flow through their own channels, each closed by its owner. That separation — data channels closed by producers, cancellation channels closed by whoever owns the cancellation — is the idiom that keeps pipelines composable.",
"The compile enforcement is a type-system story worth one layer down: chan<- T and <-chan T are distinct types, and the close operation is defined only on chan<- T (and the bidirectional chan T). When you convert a bidirectional channel to a receive-only view, the runtime value is unchanged — the same pointer — but the compile-time type no longer HAS the close method. The compiler rejects the call before any runtime question arises.\n\nIt's the same narrow-by-conversion trick as the send/receive restrictions, applied to lifecycle: the direction type says what you can DO, and close is an action reserved to the direction that owns the channel's end-of-life. Note the asymmetry with send: a <-chan can't send (compile) and can't close (compile) — the receive-only view is purely a consuming view. The chan<- (send-only) CAN close — the sender owns the end — and that's the signature of the canonical producer:\n\n```go\nfunc produce(out chan<- int) {\n    defer close(out)  // legal on chan<-, idiomatic at the producer\n    for _, v := range data {\n        out <- v\n    }\n}\n```\n\ndefer close(out) at the top of a producer is the lifecycle idiom — no matter how the function returns, the close fires, every receiver's range terminates, and the pipeline drains cleanly. That one line is the ownership model in miniature: the function that has the send authority exercises the close authority at the same scope."),
("What is the difference between a blocking send and a dropped message?",
"Go channels never drop messages — a send either blocks until delivered (or buffered) or panics on a closed channel. Dropping requires building it yourself: select with default, or a time-bounded select.",
"Channels have no drop mode. The three possible outcomes of a send, exhaustively:\n\n1. The value is delivered (unbuffered: a receiver took it; buffered: a slot holds it) — possibly after blocking.\n2. The send blocks because the channel is full or nil — the sender waits.\n3. The channel is closed — panic.\n\nThere is no fourth outcome where the value vanishes. That guarantee is what makes channels correct-by-construction for handoffs: a completed send is a delivered value, full stop.\n\nBut real systems need dropping — a metrics sink that shouldn't block the app when the collector is slow, a UI event queue that discards stale clicks. Go's answer is you build the drop with select:\n\n```go\nselect {\ncase ch <- v:\n    // delivered\n default:\n    // channel full — drop, log, count, never block the caller\n}\n```\n\nThe default case makes the select non-blocking: if no case is ready immediately, default runs. This is the idiom for bounded-queue-with-drop semantics on top of a buffered channel.\n\nThe time-bounded variant — a send that waits up to a deadline then gives up:\n\n```go\nselect {\ncase ch <- v:\n    // delivered in time\ncase <-time.After(100 * time.Millisecond):\n    // give up — timeout path\n}\n```\n\nThe interview point: the primitive is strict (block or panic), and the policy (drop, timeout, retry) is yours, built from select. Go keeps the channel honest — it will never silently lose data — and puts the lossy decisions where they belong: in explicit, visible application code.",
"The never-drop guarantee is structural: the send operation enqueues the value with the sender's goroutine state — there is no code path where a send returns without the value being committed to the channel (receiver's stack, buffer slot, or the panic). Contrast languages where queues have offer/try modes returning success flags — Go's send is a statement, not a question, and its return IS the commitment.\n\nselect with default is the non-blocking probe built into the language, and its mechanics matter: select locks ALL its channels, checks every case for readiness, and if none is ready AND a default exists, runs default — atomically, under all the locks. The try-send built this way is race-free: two goroutines both doing select-default-send on the same full channel — one wins the single free slot, the other takes default, with no lost or duplicated values, because the whole check-and-commit is under the channel locks.\n\nThat composition — one guaranteed-delivery primitive (the send) plus one atomic choice primitive (select) — generates every messaging policy: drop-when-busy (default), wait-then-drop (time.After), first-available (plain select across channels). The pattern to recognize in code review: any 'I need best-effort delivery' requirement maps to select+default over a buffered channel, and the buffer size IS the explicit tolerance for how far ahead the producer may run before losses start. The system's lossiness becomes a number you chose — not a default the runtime chose for you."),
],

"closing-channels": [
("How do you close a channel, and what does closing do?",
"close(ch) — one-time, sender-side. It wakes all receivers with the 'no more values' signal: buffered values drain first, then receives return zero+false. Range loops terminate. Send after close panics.",
"close(ch) is a broadcast with exactly one meaning: no more values will ever be sent on this channel.\n\nWhat it does, in order:\n\n- Buffered values remain receivable. The close is a tombstone placed BEHIND the queue — receivers drain the real values first.\n- After the buffer empties, every receive returns immediately with (zero value, ok=false) — forever. A closed channel is permanently 'readable and empty'.\n- All goroutines blocked receiving on the channel wake up — with buffered values if available, otherwise with the zero/false result. No receiver waits forever on a closed channel.\n- A range loop over the channel terminates when the buffered values are exhausted and close is seen.\n- Sends after close panic: `send on closed channel`.\n\nExecuted demo of the drain-then-end behavior:\n\n```go\nch := make(chan int, 1)\nch <- 10\nclose(ch)\nv, ok := <-ch   // (10, true) — buffered value delivered\nv2, ok2 := <-ch // (0, false) — channel closed and drained\n```\n\nWhat close does NOT do: it doesn't free the channel (GC does that when no references remain), doesn't interrupt in-flight sends (there are none — close means you promise not to send), and doesn't notify senders of anything (senders are the ones who called it).\n\nThe one-sentence ownership law that prevents every close bug: the goroutine that owns sending closes — after its last send, once, with defer — and nobody else.",
"Mechanically, close(ch) takes the channel lock, sets the closed flag, and then walks both wait queues — every parked receiver gets woken; every one receives either a buffered value or the end-signal. If senders are parked (they were blocked on a full channel), close wakes them too — into the panic path: a sender parked on a full channel that gets closed wakes up to `send on closed channel`. That's an edge worth knowing: the send-after-close panic isn't only for sends that START after close — a send already blocked when close lands also panics.\n\nThe tombstone-behind-the-buffer structure explains the drain order: the close operation doesn't touch the ring buffer at all — values sent before close are in the ring, and receivers take them in FIFO order as normal. The closed flag is only consulted when a receiver finds the buffer empty. So close is literally: mark + wake; the data path continues unchanged until the data runs out.\n\nThe range termination mechanic: range over a channel is a receive loop that checks ok — it's the compiler writing the comma-ok loop for you. When it receives the (zero, false), the loop exits. That's why range needs close to terminate: an open, empty channel just blocks the range forever, waiting for a value that may come. Range + close is the canonical pipeline: the producer's defer close(out) is the signal that makes every downstream range end cleanly — one line upstream, correct shutdown everywhere downstream."),
("Who should close a channel — the sender or the receiver?",
"The sender — always. Close is the producer's statement 'I will never send again'. A receiver closing races other receivers and causes send-after-close panics for innocent senders.",
"Close belongs to the send side, and the reasoning is about who KNOWS what:\n\nA receiver knows only its own consumption — it finished reading. It cannot know whether other receivers are still waiting, or whether a sender has more to produce. Its 'we're done' is a guess about everyone.\n\nThe sender knows the production-side truth: whether more values will ever exist. close() is exactly that truth, published. Only the side with the fact can broadcast the fact.\n\nThe failure modes when a receiver closes:\n\n- Another receiver, blocked on the channel, gets the end signal and exits — but it was promised a value that was still being produced. Its logic sees 'channel closed' and reports completion that never happened.\n- The actual sender, still producing, sends after the receiver's close — panic. The producer's code is correct; the receiver's close created the crash.\n\nThe multi-sender problem is the same principle harder: with several producers, no single one knows when ALL are done — so nobody closes. The standard solutions: a fan-in goroutine that receives from all producers and owns the single output channel's close; or a sync.WaitGroup tracking senders, with one closer waiting on the group before closing. In both, close is still exercised by the side with the send authority — delegated to the one place that can see all senders.\n\nThe mirror rule: receivers never close, senders never assume receivers got everything. Buffered values after close WILL drain to whoever receives. close is not a flush — it's an end marker, and the data before it flows by the normal rules.",
"The whole ownership model composes into the pipeline idiom, and seeing it as a chain of contracts makes it mechanical:\n\n```go\nfunc stage(in <-chan int, out chan<- int) {\n    defer close(out)          // this stage owns out's lifecycle\n    for v := range in {       // terminates when upstream closes\n        out <- transform(v)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nEach stage: receives on a <-chan (it can't close what it consumes — type-enforced), sends on a chan<- with defer close at the top (it owns exactly its output). Chain N stages: a close at the head propagates termination stage-by-stage — each range sees the upstream close, finishes, and its own defer closes its output — a cascade of clean shutdowns, no coordination, no cancellation logic, just lifecycle ownership at every function boundary.\n\nThe context.Context pattern is the same law, generalizing: the cancellation's OWNER (the parent request) closes Done(); consumers select on it. The owner of the fact publishes the fact — whether that's 'no more data' or 'stop working'. Go's concurrency style is a set of variations on that single principle: signal authority flows with knowledge authority, and channels (plus the close primitive, plus directional types) are the enforcement mechanism."),
("Can you close a channel twice?",
"No — the second close panics: close of closed channel. Close is one-way and one-shot by design.",
"close on an already-closed channel panics, immediately:\n\n```go\nch := make(chan int)\nclose(ch)\nclose(ch)   // panic: close of closed channel\n```\n\nThe panic text is verbatim `close of closed channel`. Same loud-failure philosophy as send-after-close: a double close means two owners each believed they held the lifecycle — an ownership bug that would otherwise silently confuse receivers (the second close's intent is unknowable: 'even MORE done'?). The panic is the stack trace pointing at the second closer.\n\nThe common real-world trigger: two goroutines both 'cleaning up' the same channel — a shutdown path that closes, then a defer that closes again (double-defer, or a defer plus an explicit close in an error branch). The fix is the single-owner discipline made mechanical: exactly ONE defer close(ch) in exactly one function — the producer — and no other close anywhere. When multiple senders exist, the fan-in or WaitGroup patterns centralize the close into one place precisely so this panic class cannot happen.\n\nThe state model makes all three close-related panics/blocks one family: closed channel + close → panic (double close); closed channel + send → panic (send after close); closed channel + receive → graceful drain then zero/false (the only gentle one, because receiving is the passive side — it can only observe). Every panic in the channel API is a lifecycle-contract violation; the design chose to make them loud rather than let lifecycle bugs become silent data-flow bugs."),
("What is the correct way to close a channel with multiple senders?",
"Centralize: one owner closes. Either a fan-in goroutine that merges all senders and owns the output's close, or a WaitGroup over senders with a closer that waits. No sender ever closes 'its own' shared channel.",
"A shared channel with multiple senders has a coordination problem: close means 'no one will ever send again', but each sender knows only its own intent. The two canonical solutions:\n\nFan-in pattern — a dedicated merger owns the output:\n\n```go\nfunc merge(outputs ...<-chan int) <-chan int {\n    out := make(chan int)\n    var wg sync.WaitGroup\n    wg.Add(len(outputs))\n    for _, ch := range outputs {\n        go func(c <-chan int) {\n            defer wg.Done()\n            for v := range c {   // each input closes normally\n                out <- v\n            }\n        }(ch)\n    }\n    go func() {\n        wg.Wait()      // ALL forwarding goroutines done\n        close(out)    // now, and only now: nobody will send\n    }()\n    return out\n}\n```\n\nEach upstream producer closes its own channel (its prerogative — it's the sole sender there). The merge goroutines drain those inputs; the closer waits for every one to finish, then closes the single output once. Exactly one close of the shared channel, executed by the one goroutine that has observed every sender finish.\n\nWaitGroup-at-source pattern — when the senders write directly into a shared channel:\n\n```go\nvar wg sync.WaitGroup\nfor i := 0; i < 3; i++ {\n    wg.Add(1)\n    go func(id int) {\n        defer wg.Done()\n        for _, v := range work(id) {\n            results <- v\n        }\n    }(i)\n}\ngo func() {\n    wg.Wait()\n    close(results)\n}()\n```\n\nSame law, different placement: the closer waits on the count of all senders. What both patterns share — and what the interview answer should name — is that 'who closes' is answered by 'who can PROVE production ended', and with multiple producers, proof is a synchronization primitive (WaitGroup) or an architecture (fan-in), never a per-sender close.",
"The underlying synchronization both patterns build is a happens-before chain that the close semantics require: the close must happen-after every send, and receivers' end-signal observation must happen-after the close. The WaitGroup gives you the first edge: each sender's Done() happens-before Wait() returns — so all sends happen-before the close in the closer goroutine. No send can race the close, so no send-after-close panic can occur, and no buffered value can be lost after close (close is only called with the queue already final).\n\nThe fan-in variant composes the same edge through channel closes: each upstream close happens-before the forwarding goroutine's range-termination, each termination happens-before its Done, all Dones happen-before Wait returns, which happens-before the output close. It's the same chain, one hop longer — which is why pipelines built entirely of close-terminating stages shut down so cleanly: every hop is a happens-before edge the runtime guarantees.\n\nThe alternative people reach for before learning these patterns — a shared 'done' flag channel that senders select on — is solving a DIFFERENT problem (cancellation: stopping senders early) and doesn't answer the close question at all. The two problems compose: cancellation (context/select) for early exit, the single-owner close for normal completion. Production Go uses both, and confusing them is the top source of pipeline deadlocks — a cancelled sender that never lets the WaitGroup reach zero, so the closer never fires, so receivers block forever. The complete pattern: senders select on ctx.Done() AND wg.Done() on every exit path."),
("What happens to buffered values when you close a channel?",
"They remain receivable — close drains first, ends second. Receivers get every buffered value with ok=true, then zero+false after the buffer empties.",
"Close places the end marker behind the queue, not in place of it:\n\n```go\nch := make(chan int, 3)\nch <- 1\nch <- 2\nclose(ch)\n\nfmt.Println(<-ch) // 1 — buffered value, normal receive\nfmt.Println(<-ch) // 2 — buffered value, normal receive\nv, ok := <-ch     // 0, false — queue drained, now the end signal\n```\n\nExecuted order: the two real values arrive first; only after the buffer empties does the (zero, false) end-signal appear. A range loop over that channel receives 1, 2, then terminates — close guarantees the range sees every value sent before it, then stops.\n\nThis is the semantics that make pipelines reliable: a producer's defer close(out) never loses data. Whatever was already in flight (buffered) reaches consumers; the close just adds the ending. The composition 'send everything, then close' and 'range until close' forms a lossless handoff with an explicit termination — the reason channel pipelines can be built without any acknowledgment protocol.\n\nOne asymmetry to close the loop: unbuffered channels have nothing to drain — close is immediately visible to every receiver as the end signal (verified: an unbuffered channel closed then received gives (zero-value, false) on the first receive, no buffered phase). The drain-then-end behavior belongs to buffered channels; the end-immediately behavior to unbuffered ones. Both are 'no value is ever lost' — the difference is just whether there was a queue in front of the tombstone."),
],

"range-over-channel": [
("How does range work with a channel, and why does it need close?",
"for v := range ch receives until the channel is closed AND drained, then terminates. Without a close, an empty channel blocks the range forever — range has no other exit.",
"range over a channel is a receive loop the compiler writes for you:\n\n```go\nfor v := range ch {\n    process(v)\n}\n```\n\nEach iteration is a receive; the loop ends when the channel delivers the end-signal — closed and drained. There is NO other exit: no condition clause, no break-on-error, nothing to configure. The loop either runs until close or blocks forever on an empty open channel.\n\nThat's why range over channels requires the close discipline: the producer MUST close (typically defer close at the top of the producing function), or every downstream range leaks into a permanent block. The pairing is the idiom:\n\n```go\nfunc produce(out chan<- int) {\n    defer close(out)  // the range's termination signal\n    for _, v := range data {\n        out <- v\n    }\n}\n\n// consumer\nfor v := range out {   // terminates exactly when produce returns\n    use(v)\n}\n```\n\nThe mechanics under the loop: range is the comma-ok receive — each iteration does v, ok := <-ch; ok true → body; ok false → exit. Buffered values drain normally (ok true for each); the close is seen only when the buffer empties. So range gets the lossless-drain-then-terminate guarantee the close semantics provide.\n\nWhere range DOESN'T fit: when the consumer has multiple channels, needs timeouts, or wants to stop early — those are select's jobs, with the comma-ok form explicitly written. range is the single-channel, run-to-completion loop — the simplest correct consumer, provided the producer owns the close."),
("Can you break out of a range loop over a channel early?",
"Yes — break exits the range like any loop. The channel stays open, values keep buffering (up to capacity), and the goroutine that would send into it can block later. Early exit needs a cancellation signal if you want senders to stop too.",
"break works exactly as you'd hope:\n\n```go\nfor v := range ch {\n    if v == stopValue {\n        break   // leaves the range; ch is still open\n    }\n    process(v)\n}\n```\n\nThe range ends, but the channel's life continues. The consequences differ by what's upstream:\n\nUnbuffered channel with a sender blocked trying to deliver: your break leaves that sender parked on its send — a goroutine leak. The sender needs to see a cancellation (a done channel, a context) to give up.\n\nBuffered channel: subsequent sends fill the buffer (up to capacity), then senders block. Values you didn't consume are discarded when the channel is garbage-collected — but a sender blocked on a full channel leaks exactly as the unbuffered case.\n\nSo break answers YOUR side ('stop consuming') but not THEIRS ('stop producing'). The complete early-exit pattern pairs the break with a cancellation the producer selects on:\n\n```go\ndone := make(chan struct{})\n// producer: for { select { case out <- v: case <-done: return } }\n\nfor v := range out {\n    if !keepGoing(v) {\n        close(done)  // tell the producer\n        break        // and leave\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThat's the two-sided exit: consumer breaks, producer observes the done close, unwinds. context.Context is this same pattern, standardized — Done() is the done channel, and the idiom above is why ctx appears in every pipeline signature."),
("What is the difference between range over a channel and a for loop with receive?",
"range is the run-to-completion form: receive until closed, nothing else. A for loop with explicit receives can do anything per-receive — conditions, comma-ok, multiple channels — at the cost of writing the termination yourself.",
"The two forms, side by side, doing the same job:\n\n```go\n// range — the specialist\nfor v := range ch {\n    process(v)\n}\n\n// for + receive — the general form\nfor {\n    v, ok := <-ch\n    if !ok {\n        break\n    }\n    process(v)\n}\n```\n\nThe range is the second loop, compiled: receive, check ok, run body, exit on end-signal. Every behavior difference falls out of that:\n\nrange has no per-iteration condition — you can't 'stop when v satisfies X' inside the range declaration itself (break in the body does it, but then the loop's exit is two mechanisms: close OR break). The for form has one explicit exit you fully own.\n\nrange can't select — one channel only. The for form composes with anything: select across multiple channels, timeouts, comma-ok — the full consumer toolkit.\n\nThe practical divide: range for the standard pipeline consumer — single upstream channel, run until the producer closes, the 90% case; it's less code and reads as 'drain this source.' The explicit loop when the consumer is a coordinator: multiple sources, deadlines, early exits, per-receive branching beyond processing. Code review often flags the reverse: an explicit comma-ok loop with no special logic is noise — range says it cleaner — and a range with three breaks and a condition hidden in the body is a loop that wants to be explicit."),
("Does range over a channel receive buffered values after close?",
"Yes — range drains every buffered value first (each yielded normally), then sees the close and terminates. No value sent before close is ever skipped.",
"The order is queue-then-tombstone, and range follows it exactly:\n\n```go\nch := make(chan int, 3)\nch <- 1\nch <- 2\nch <- 3\nclose(ch)\n\nfor v := range ch {\n    fmt.Println(v)   // 1, 2, 3 — all three, then the loop ends\n}\n```\n\nExecuted: three values print, then the range terminates. The close never truncates the queue — it marks the end AFTER everything already sent. This losslessness is the property that makes defer close(out) safe in every producer: the values in flight at close time reach the consumer; the close only adds the ending.\n\nIt composes into the pipeline shutdown cascade: stage A finishes and closes its output; stage B's range receives A's remaining buffered values, processes them all, exits its own loop, and its defer closes B's output — the termination flows downstream WITH the data draining ahead of it. Every stage sees: all the data, then the end, then it passes both along. No dropped tail, no race between 'last value' and 'close' — the channel's own ordering guarantees the sequence."),
("How does range interact with a nil channel?",
"A nil channel in a range blocks forever on the first receive — same as any nil receive. range never sees values or termination; the goroutine parks permanently.",
"range over a nil channel is the nil-receive behavior on loop:\n\n```go\nvar ch chan int          // nil\nfor v := range ch {      // first receive: block forever\n    process(v)           // never runs\n}\n```\n\nThe goroutine parks on the first iteration's receive and never wakes — a leak with a stack trace showing the park. If every other goroutine also blocks, the runtime's deadlock detector fires; if anything else runs, the leak is silent.\n\nThe classic cause: a channel field or variable declared but never make'd, passed into a range-driven consumer. The compiler can't catch it (the nil channel is a runtime state), so it surfaces as 'this worker never processes anything' — and the diagnostic is the goroutine dump: the parked receive shows the nil channel in its frame.\n\nOne legitimate use, inherited from the nil-receive rules: a range over a channel variable that BECOMES nil mid-loop (set to nil elsewhere, race aside) would block at the next receive — the loop can't be disabled that way cleanly. The select-based nil trick (nil disables a select CASE) doesn't transfer to range; range is single-channel and total. If you need 'stop consuming this source temporarily', you're outside range's design — select with the channel plus a control channel is the shape."),
],

"goroutine-vs-thread": [
("What is the difference between a goroutine and an OS thread?",
"Goroutines are runtime-managed, multiplexed onto OS threads by the Go scheduler — ~2KB stacks, cheap creation (100k in ~90ms). OS threads are kernel-managed, ~1-8MB stacks, expensive creation. You don't run 100k OS threads on one machine.",
"A goroutine is a function running concurrently under the Go runtime's management; an OS thread is the kernel's unit of scheduling. The Go scheduler multiplexes many goroutines onto few OS threads — M:N scheduling — and that's the entire difference in one sentence. Everything practical follows:\n\nCost: a goroutine starts with a ~2KB stack (grows/shrinks as needed); an OS thread commits ~1-8MB of stack plus kernel bookkeeping. Executed proof: starting and finishing 100,000 goroutines took ~90ms on a modest machine — try 100,000 threads and the machine falls over (thread limits, scheduler collapse, memory).\n\nScheduling: the Go scheduler runs in userspace — goroutine switches are function calls the runtime makes (hundreds of nanoseconds), no kernel involvement. Thread switches go through the kernel (microseconds, plus cache pollution). The Go scheduler also switches at channel operations and function calls — places where a goroutine is about to block anyway — making the common case cooperative and cheap.\n\nBlocking behavior: a goroutine blocking on a channel or syscall doesn't block its OS thread (the runtime parks the goroutine and moves the thread to other work — or spins up another thread for the syscall). A blocked OS thread IS blocked, consuming its resources.\n\nScale: because of all the above, Go code routinely runs servers with hundreds of thousands of concurrent goroutines — one per connection — which is the architectural style the whole ecosystem (net/http) is built around. The answer interviewers want is the M:N model with the cost numbers that make the concurrency style viable."),
("How does the Go scheduler work (G-M-P)?",
"G = goroutine, M = machine (OS thread), P = processor (scheduler context, holds a run queue). The scheduler multiplexes Gs onto Ms through Ps — stealing work when a P runs dry.",
"The model, in its three letters:\n\nG — goroutine: the work item. Each G has its own stack and state, parked when blocked on channels/mutexes/timers.\n\nM — machine: an OS thread. Ms are what actually execute — the runtime creates them as needed (and the OS caps them, hence the M is the scarce resource).\n\nP — processor: the scheduling context that an M must hold to execute Go code. Each P has a local run queue of Gs ready to run. GOMAXPROCS sets the number of Ps — by default the CPU core count — which is why it bounds true parallelism: at most GOMAXPROCS Ms are executing Go code at once.\n\nThe dynamics that make the model work:\n\nWork stealing: when a P's local queue empties, it steals Gs from another P's queue (half of a victim's queue) or checks global queues. This keeps all Ps busy without a central bottleneck.\n\nHandoff on block: when a goroutine blocks on a channel, the runtime parks the G and the M (via its P) picks another G — the M never idles on a blocked G. When a goroutine enters a syscall, the M goes with it (the syscall owns the thread); the P is handed to another M so other Gs keep running. That's why blocking syscalls don't stall the machine in Go.\n\nPreemption: since Go 1.14, the scheduler preempts long-running Gs at function calls (async preemption via signals) — no goroutine can hog a P forever, closing the loop for schedulability guarantees.\n\nThe number that anchors it: runtime.GOMAXPROCS(0) on a 16-core box prints 16 — 16 Ps, any number of Ms (grown on demand), and the hundreds of thousands of Gs distributed across the P queues."),
("Why are goroutine stacks so small — how do they grow?",
"Goroutines start at ~2KB with a contiguous, growable stack: when a function call needs more, the runtime allocates a bigger block, copies the stack over, and redirects — all invisible to the code.",
"The 2KB start: goroutines begin with a small contiguous stack — enough for a typical call chain — because most goroutines never need more, and starting small is what makes 'a goroutine per request, per connection, per item' affordable at 100k+ scale.\n\nGrowing: the runtime checks stack space at function entry (a small prologue comparing the stack pointer to a guard). When a call would overflow, the runtime:\n\n1. Allocates a new stack — typically double the current size (2KB → 4KB → 8KB...).\n2. Copies the entire existing stack contents to the new block.\n3. Rewrites all pointers INTO the stack (locals, spill slots) to the new addresses.\n4. Frees the old block and continues.\n\nThe whole operation is transparent — running code has no idea its stack moved. This works precisely because Go's runtime knows everything about the stack: it controls the frame layout, tracks pointer-containing slots (that's what escape analysis and the frame-pointer maps are FOR), and can therefore adjust every reference. C can't do this — stacks in C are fixed-at-create because arbitrary pointers into them exist and can't be found.\n\nThe shrink: when a deep call returns, a goroutine's stack can shrink back down (a GC-time optimization), so a goroutine that briefly went deep doesn't hold that memory forever.\n\nThe consequence for interview answers: goroutines cost 2KB + whatever your call depth actually grows to — the initial number is a floor, not a cap. Deep recursion costs real memory per goroutine, same as threads, but the common case (shallow handlers, parked waits) stays near the floor — which is exactly the pattern the concurrency style encourages."),
("What happens to goroutines when main returns?",
"main returning ends the program — immediately, all goroutines, no defers elsewhere run, no 'wait for children'. Any goroutine still needed must be joined (WaitGroup/channel) before main exits.",
"The rule is absolute: when the main goroutine returns from main, the program exits. Every other goroutine — mid-computation, mid-send, holding a half-written file — is simply gone. No cleanup, no cancellation callback, no final chance. The runtime doesn't attempt a shutdown handshake; it terminates.\n\nThat's why 'does main wait for goroutines?' is the wrong question — it doesn't, and by design: most programs have goroutines that may never finish (servers, listeners, pollers); waiting for them all would mean most programs never exit. Exit is main's decision, made when main's work is done.\n\nThe discipline that follows: any goroutine whose WORK matters to the outcome must be joined before main returns:\n\n```go\nvar wg sync.WaitGroup\nfor _, task := range tasks {\n    wg.Add(1)\n    go func(t Task) { defer wg.Done(); run(t) }(task)\n}\nwg.Wait()   // main blocks here — the results exist\n```\n\nor the channel form — receive the results you need:\n\n```go\nresults := make(chan Result, len(tasks))\n// ... goroutines send into results ...\nfor i := 0; i < len(tasks); i++ {\n    use(<-results)   // main consumes until it has everything\n}\n```\n\nThe bug to recognize: fire-and-forget goroutines doing necessary work (logging, flushing, writing) — the program looks correct in tests (fast goroutines finish before main) and intermittently loses output in production (main races them). If the work matters, main must wait for it; if main needn't wait, the work didn't matter."),
("When would you use a goroutine vs just calling the function?",
"Use a goroutine when the work should run concurrently with the caller — overlap with other work, run independent of the request path, or handle multiple things at once. If the caller needs the result immediately, a plain call IS the answer.",
"Start from the caller: does the code after the call need the result? Then just call it — a plain function call is simpler, faster (no scheduling, no stack switch), and the data flow is obvious:\n\n```go\nresult := compute()   // sequential, clear\nuse(result)\n```\n\nMaking that a goroutine would mean waiting on a channel for the value — same sequence, more machinery, no benefit.\n\nReach for a goroutine when the answer to 'must the caller have the result next?' is no:\n\n- Overlap: the work takes time and other useful work can proceed — a server handling the next request, a computation while IO is in flight. go lets the current path continue immediately.\n- Independence: the work isn't part of the request path at all — background cleanup, metrics flush, cache refresh. The goroutine owns its lifecycle (with a cancellation story), the caller moves on.\n- Multiplicity: N of the same thing at once — fan-out queries, per-connection handlers, parallel stages of a job. The goroutine-per-item pattern is the reason goroutines are cheap enough to spawn unthinkingly.\n\nThe diagnostic question that catches most misuse: 'what does the goroutine's OUTPUT affect, and when?' If the answer is 'the caller, right now' — wrong tool, call the function. If it's 'nothing in this path' (fire-and-forget, needs a failure story) or 'the caller, later, via channel' (async result, needs the receive side designed) — goroutine. The concurrency is never free: every go statement creates a synchronization question (how do we see its result / know it's done / stop it), and answering 'we don't need to' honestly is what separates deliberate goroutines from concurrency spam."),
],
}
